---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-coffee`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Coffee',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Coffee', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Coffee: Does Caffeine Affect Absorption or Levels?"
  description="Can you take vitamin D with coffee? Does caffeine deplete it? Separating the weak observational signal from the practical reality of how to time your supplement around your morning brew."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-coffee", label: "Vitamin D & Coffee" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Coffee</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      A popular worry is that your morning coffee is quietly cancelling out your vitamin D. The claim
      turns up constantly online. The reality is far less dramatic: coffee is not meaningfully
      "depleting" your vitamin D, and the only genuinely practical point is that taking a supplement
      with a black coffee — and nothing else — misses the absorption boost you get from food.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Where the concern comes from</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Two threads feed the idea. First, some older laboratory and observational work suggested caffeine
      might down-regulate the vitamin D receptor (VDR) in certain cells, or that heavy coffee intake
      correlated with slightly lower bone density in some studies. Second, coffee has a mild diuretic
      effect and increases urinary calcium a little, which — in the context of a very low calcium intake
      — could theoretically matter for bones. Neither of these amounts to coffee "destroying" your
      vitamin D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the evidence actually supports</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      In practice, moderate coffee drinking (up to ~3–4 cups a day) has not been shown to cause vitamin D
      deficiency or to appreciably lower 25(OH)D in well-nourished people. The receptor findings are
      cell-level signals, not evidence of a clinically important effect at normal intakes. The small
      calcium-loss effect of caffeine is easily offset by an adequate calcium intake and is negligible
      for most people. In short, your coffee habit is not the reason your vitamin D is low.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The one thing worth changing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The practical issue is not caffeine — it is <em>what you take the supplement with</em>. If your
      routine is "vitamin D with a black coffee on an empty stomach," you are missing the roughly 50%
      absorption gain from taking it with a fatty meal (see
      <a href="/vitamin-d-with-or-without-food" class="text-amber-700 underline">with or without food</a>).
      Coffee itself contains no fat to help absorption. So the fix is not to avoid coffee — it is to take
      your vitamin D with breakfast, or with your largest meal of the day, whether or not coffee is also
      on the table.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bottom line</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Moderate coffee does not meaningfully deplete vitamin D — this is largely a myth.</li>
      <li>You can drink coffee and take vitamin D; there is no direct interaction to fear.</li>
      <li>Just take the supplement with a meal containing fat, not with coffee alone, for best absorption.</li>
      <li>If you drink a lot of coffee, make sure your calcium intake is adequate for bone health.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Coffee is not why your vitamin D is low. If your level is
      poor, look at dose, sun exposure, and whether you take it with food — see our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-dosage-calculator" class="underline">dosage calculator</a>.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-coffee" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
